            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>The Problem: High-Volume Creatives That Don't Convert</h2>
<p>"This creative has a 6% CTR, running strong—yet ROI is in the red."</p>
<p>This is a common scenario in performance marketing. High-volume creatives and high-converting creatives are two different things. One solves "will users click," the other solves "will users buy."</p>
<p>The purpose of a creative review is to separate these dimensions and find creatives worth scaling.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Creative Review Checklist: 3 Steps</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Filter Review-Eligible Creatives</h3>
<p><strong>Prerequisite</strong>: Creatives must run for at least 7 days with total spend ≥ 3× daily budget.</p>
<p>Checklist:<br />
- [ ] Total impressions ≥ 10,000<br />
- [ ] Total clicks ≥ 200<br />
- [ ] Corresponding conversions ≥ 10<br />
- [ ] Exclude first 3-day "newness spike" data</p>
<hr />
<h3>Step 2: Horizontal Data Comparison</h3>
<p>Pull all creatives under the same ad set and compare these metrics:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Check Item</th>
<th>Formula</th>
<th>Benchmark</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CTR</td>
<td>Clicks / Impressions × 100%</td>
<td>1.5× above ad set average = "volume"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CVR</td>
<td>Conversions / Clicks × 100%</td>
<td>2× above average = "convert"</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPA</td>
<td>Spend / Conversions</td>
<td>Below target CPA</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Efficiency Score</td>
<td>CTR × CVR × 1000</td>
<td>Higher = priority to keep</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical insight</strong>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A utility app tested Creative A (CTR 4.8%, CVR 0.4%) vs Creative B (CTR 2.1%, CVR 2.2%).</p>
<p>Efficiency scores: A=19.2, B=46.2. Creative B's CPA was 38% lower despite lower click volume.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h3>Step 3: Conversion Funnel Analysis</h3>
<p>Final conversion numbers aren't enough—break down each stage:</p>
<p>Checklist:<br />
- [ ] Click → Landing page load (check page speed)<br />
- [ ] Landing page → Add-to-cart/Signup (check creative-to-page relevance)<br />
- [ ] Add-to-cart → Purchase (check payment flow)</p>
<p>If users drop immediately after landing page load → <strong>creative attracts wrong audience</strong>. If drop-off happens at checkout → <strong>product page or pricing issue</strong>, not creative.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Post-Review Action Plan</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Creative Type</th>
<th>Pattern</th>
<th>Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>High-volume, low-convert</td>
<td>High CTR, low CVR, drop at landing</td>
<td>Refine targeting or optimize landing page</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>High-convert, low-volume</td>
<td>Low CTR, high CVR, insufficient impressions</td>
<td>Test new placements or increase budget</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Both strong</td>
<td>High CTR + high CVR</td>
<td>Scale immediately, prioritize budget</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Both weak</td>
<td>Low CTR + low CVR</td>
<td>Pause, investigate other variables</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>Review Frequency</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>New creatives</strong>: First review at Day 7, optimize or pause underperformers early</li>
<li><strong>Top performers</strong>: Weekly horizontal comparison, watch for CTR decay</li>
<li><strong>Stable creatives</strong>: Bi-weekly funnel analysis to catch traffic quality drop</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>High-volume ≠ high-converting. High-converting ≠ high-volume.</p>
<p>Separate CTR from CVR in your reviews, use funnel analysis to identify drop-off points, then match actions to findings. The core principle: <strong>let data drive decisions, not gut feeling interpreting data</strong>.</p>
<p>One actionable step beats a perfect report every time.</p>
